ggml-cpu: tiled flash attention for small batches (2-63 queries), with heads that share K/V in one tile

Speculative verify batches (2-9 tokens) took the per-row one_chunk path: it rounds Q to FP16, so a verify batch
scored differently from the prefill of the same tokens, and every query row re-read and re-converted the whole KV.
Now they take the tiled path, with these changes:
- the GEMMs, scaling and softmax run on the valid rows of a tile only (they ran on all Q_TILE_SZ rows);
- a tile spans the heads that share one K/V head (rows run head-major), so each K/V tile is converted once per
  group instead of once per head;
- small batches get one chunk per thread, aligned to whole groups when there are at least as many groups as threads;
- below 16 rows per chunk the per-row path stays: too few rows to repay the K/V conversion (Ling, 2 tokens, 4
  threads: 10% slower without this gate).
Single-token decode keeps the split-KV path.
x86 (AVX2, 4 threads), depth 4000, ms per batch, before -> after: MiniCPM5 batch 2 55.8 -> 56.2, batch 4
86.1 -> 65.8, batch 8 167.3 -> 103.8; Ling 32.0 -> 32.3, 48.4 -> 49.2, 83.4 -> 67.9; decode and prefill 512
unchanged. Numerics: MiniCPM5 perplexity with 4- and 8-token ubatches against 512-token ubatches, KL mean 0.000000,
max 5.9e-5, same top token 100% (stock with 4-token ubatches: mean 0.0013, 98.2%). Phone speed: not measured yet.
